Looking at the areas: each small square piece covers 4 unit squares, and each trapezoid piece covers 7 unit squares (2 + 4 + two half‑squares). The boat has area 12 (cabin) + 13 (upper hull row) + 11 (lower hull row) = 36 unit squares.

Since five shapes cover at most 5 × 7 = 35 < 36 units, at least six shapes are needed. Six shapes can achieve exactly 36 units only as 4 trapezoids (28) + 2 squares (8), and such an arrangement can be laid out on the boat (trapezoids handling the slanted hull ends and middle, squares filling the cabin), so six shapes suffice.

\boxed{6}
